<td id="S4.T1.1.1.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">16.75</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.1.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.1.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Attention}_{\text{QKV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Attention</mtext><mtext>QKV</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Attention}_{\text{QKV}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T1.1.1.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_t">30.48 %</td>
<td id="S4.T1.1.1.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_t">11.11</td>
<td id="S4.T1.1.1.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_t">30.19 %</td>
<td id="S4.T1.1.1.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_t">18.31</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>ETT Target and corresponding LongBench scores for Experiment GPT-Large and Phi-2.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>The shallower key layers are less effective compared to the deeper layers
</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also empirically investigate the effectiveness of fine-tuning shallower <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FFN}_{\text{Up}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>FFN</mtext><mtext>Up</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FFN}_{\text{Up}}</annotation></semantics></math> layers at test-time. If we freeze a block of shalloest layer and observe no impact on <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ETT" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ETT</annotation></semantics></math>’s performance, it suggests that the layers are not essential for <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ETT" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ETT</annotation></semantics></math>. To identify the optimal block of shallow layers to freeze, we incrementally freeze shallow layers and evaluate <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ETT" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ETT</annotation></semantics></math>’s performance at each step. This bottom-up strategy reduces the number of trainable parameters and computational cost, as backpropagation is not required for the shallow frozen layers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.3 The shallower key layers are less effective compared to the deeper layers ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ ETT: Expanding the Long Context Understanding Capability of LLMs at Test-Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ETT" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ETT</annotation></semantics></math>’s average LongBench score as the fraction of shallow key (<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FFN}_{\text{Up}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>FFN</mtext><mtext>Up</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FFN}_{\text{Up}}</annotation></semantics></math>) layers frozen. We observe that fine-tuning only the top 80% of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FFN}_{\text{Up}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>FFN</mtext><mtext>Up</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FFN}_{\text{Up}}</annotation></semantics></math> layers achieves the similar performance as fine-tuning all layers. Importantly, there is a sharp performance degradation when freezing more than 40% of the shallow layers, indicating a transition point beyond which key contextual information is no longer preserved.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.06313v1/figures/Prunning__GPT.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/247;" width="333" height="247" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><math id="S4.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ETT" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ETT</annotation></semantics></math>’s LongBench score as a function of the fraction of deep <math id="S4.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FFN}_{\text{Up}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>FFN</mtext><mtext>Up</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FFN}_{\text{Up}}</annotation></semantics></math> layers finetuned. We can sore the long input in the top 60% of <math id="S4.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FFN}_{\text{Up}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>FFN</mtext><mtext>Up</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FFN}_{\text{Up}}</annotation></semantics></math> layers without significant performance degradation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The LongBench scores for GPT-Large and Phi-2, with and without the parameter-efficient version of <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ETT" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ETT</annotation></semantics></math>, are reported in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 The shallower key layers are less effective compared to the deeper layers ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ ETT: Expanding the Long Context Understanding Capability of LLMs at Test-Time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. In all experiments, we fine-tuned the top 80% of the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FFN}_{\text{Up}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>FFN</mtext><mtext>Up</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FFN}_{\text{Up}}</annotation></semantics></math> layers.</p>
</div>
